[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1582","stub":{"id":"1582","section":"Route 259 to Saylor Park in Blacklick","altname":"","river":"Blacklick Creek","difficulty":"IItoIIIstandoutIV","states":["USA-PEN"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"383","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"109136","location":{"latitude":"40.4742","longitude":"-79.092"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"waterfall","description":"About 3/4 of a mile below the putin the stream bed constricts at a boulder strewn ledge called Auld's Run Rapid. So called because of the entry of Auld's Run from river right before the drop. This small tributary is easy to spot due to its bright orange color. After this, also on the right there is a good scouting eddy just above the drop. It is possible to beach here to scout or portage as well. The cleanest line is a diagonal across the rapid from the eddy dropping over on the left by the leaning boulder.\n\n[http://youtu.be/hhoypmgPCt4](http://youtu.be/hhoypmgPCt4)","id":"100639","location":{"latitude":"40.47169876098633","longitude":"-79.10810089111328"},"name":"Auld's Run","distance":"0.8","difficulty":"III+","approximate":false,"photoID":"42418"},{"type":"waterfall","description":"The photo shows the right channel. The big square rock in mid channel, at the bottom is Jack's Rock. It is slightly undercut, and has logs across the bottom of both routes around the rock that will be there for years. Although it is possible to paddle over the logs, the danger of entrapment for swimmers is very high. The left channel of the island is a fun and safe route.","id":"100850","location":{"latitude":"40.47309875488281","longitude":"-79.12120056152344"},"name":"Jacks Rock","distance":"1.8","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"702973"},{"type":"waterfall","description":"The left channel of this rapid ends in a boulder sive. So, enter this rapid right of the two mid stream boulders. Stay left and above the drop until you are past the first diagonal wave-hole then run down the left.   [http://youtu.be/yACU5PDjr48](http://youtu.be/yACU5PDjr48)","id":"100641","location":{"latitude":"40.474300384521484","longitude":"-79.13980102539062"},"name":"Diagonal Ledges (Surfers) ","distance":"3.2","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"702969"},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"114373","location":{"latitude":"40.475","longitude":"-79.187"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"6.2","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"100824206","title":null,"post_date":"2011-04-26 04:00:00+00","detail":"Ran Blacklick 5/3/09, approx. 2 miles below Heshbon there is an island. A big log remains stuck on the very large square rock in the center(Jack's Rock), at the bottom of the right channel. The log is across the passages on both sides of the rock. The right channel of this island should not be run. The left channelis open. There are pictures of this hazard in the photo sec.","user":{"name":"Randall McFarland"}}],"photos":[{"id":"40458","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9600","description":"This is the log at the bottom of the right channel of the large island below Heshbon.\r\nThe left channel is open.","caption":"Nasty Wood","poi_name":"Center Rock","subject":"","author":"Rand McFarland","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/40458","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/40458"}},{"id":"702973","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"702974","description":"This is the rt. channel with the submerged logs across big square & undercut rock  (Jack's Rock) viewed from upstream, take the left channel instead it's more fun anyway.","caption":"Jack's Rock","poi_name":"Jack's Rock","subject":"","author":"Rand McFarland (KHCC)","poi_id":"100850","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/702973","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/702973"}},{"id":"873332","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873333","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873332","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873332"}},{"id":"743145","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"743135","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Rand McFarland (KHCC)","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/743145","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/743145"}},{"id":"873344","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873345","description":"","caption":"Woo,Hoo!!!","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873344","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873344"}},{"id":"873348","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873349","description":"","caption":"Learning To Fly.","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873348","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873348"}},{"id":"873328","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873329","description":"Ooops!","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873328","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873328"}},{"id":"873310","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873311","description":"Just A Ahead Of The Ice Flow, Riding The Swell To Saylor."